# US terminology and word choice (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-09-27), verbatim:

> **Use "tier" rather than "band". Use environment or site(s) rather than
> estate. Do not use the word honesty. Do not estimate completion times of
> projects. Stop using UK and EU terminology and linguistics. Use only USA
> linguistics.**

## The rule

> **Write United States English only. Use tier, never band. Use
> environment or site, never estate. Never write the word honesty or its
> forms. Never state how long a project will take. This binds every
> authored surface: documents, operator user interface strings, commit
> bodies, plans, contracts, and chat replies.**

## 1. Required substitutions

| Never write | Always write |
| --- | --- |
| band, bands, banding, volume band, size band, price band | **tier**, tiers, tiering, volume tier, price tier |
| estate, estates | **environment**, environments, or **site**, sites |
| honesty, honest, honestly, dishonest | Delete the sentence, or state the fact. See section 2 |
| "about 6 months to deliver", "3 to 6 month rollout", "ready by Q3" | Nothing. See section 3 |

"Tier" already matches how the market prices, so this also satisfies
[`competitor-vocabulary-commercial-only.mdc`](competitor-vocabulary-commercial-only.mdc),
which independently forbids band and estate as coined product labels.

## 2. The word honesty is banned outright

Do not write honesty, honest, honestly, or dishonest anywhere. The word
signals a document arguing with itself, and it is the musing pattern
[`competitor-vocabulary-commercial-only.mdc`](competitor-vocabulary-commercial-only.mdc)
already bans from commercial papers. This rule extends the ban to every
surface, including internal notes and chat.

| Instead of | Write |
| --- | --- |
| "with an honest status per row" | "with the current status per row" |
| "Honesty caveat: these are estimates" | "Caveat: these are estimates" |
| "Where we do not win, stated honestly" | "Where we do not win" |
| "an honest assessment of the gap" | "the measured gap" |
| "to be honest, this is unverified" | "this is unverified" |

**What does not change.** The underlying discipline is unaffected. Keep
reporting Live, Partial, Absent, or BLOCKED; keep labeling estimates as
estimates; keep saying what was not verified. State the fact and drop the
adjective. Accuracy obligations continue under
[`compliance-claims-honesty.mdc`](compliance-claims-honesty.mdc), whose
filename is a path and not prose.

## 3. No project completion-time estimates

Do not predict how long work will take. This covers our own work and any
third party's.

| Forbidden | Allowed |
| --- | --- |
| "about 3 months to implement" | The ordered list of slices, with no durations |
| "Phase 2 lands in Q4" | "Phase 2 follows Phase 1" |
| "a 2 to 4 month rollout" for a competitor | The competitor's meter, packaging, and price |
| "12 to 18 months to reach maturity" | The capability that is missing |
| "this should take a week" | What remains, and what gates it |

**Not a duration estimate, and still allowed:** a billing or subscription
period such as Year 1, a contract term, a certificate validity period such
as 47 days, a renewal window, a review cycle, a retention period, a
regulatory effective date already published by an authority, and a
timestamp recording when something actually happened.

Sequencing is allowed. Dating the sequence is not.

## 4. United States linguistics only

No United Kingdom or European Union spelling, vocabulary, or idiom. The
spelling list in
[`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) section 1 still
applies in full. This rule adds vocabulary:

| Never | Always |
| --- | --- |
| estate | environment, site |
| whilst, amongst | while, among |
| bespoke | custom |
| turnover (revenue sense) | revenue |
| licence (noun) | license |
| organisation, recognise, analyse, minimise, prioritise, utilise | organization, recognize, analyze, minimize, prioritize, use |
| centre, programme, catalogue, defence, favour, behaviour, colour, grey | center, program, catalog, defense, favor, behavior, color, gray |
| enquiry | inquiry |
| post code | ZIP code |
| mobile (phone sense) | cell phone |
| different to | different from |
| learnt | learned |

**Quoting a source that uses non-US spelling.** Paraphrase into United
States English instead of quoting. If the exact wording matters, quote it
and leave the source spelling inside the quotation marks only.

## Forbidden constructs

- The words band, estate, honesty, or any form of honest, in authored
  content.
- Any sentence predicting how long a project, phase, migration, rollout,
  or implementation will take, including a competitor's.
- United Kingdom or European Union spelling or vocabulary outside a
  marked direct quotation.
- Renaming a shipped identifier to satisfy this rule. Code tokens, table
  names, route paths, and existing rule filenames keep their spelling;
  this rule governs prose, user interface strings, and new identifiers.

## Worked violation to fix

**Violation:**

```text
## Large estate: 6,400 managed names

Indicative bands for the estate below. Reported implementation timelines
are 3 to 6 months for the incumbent. Honesty caveat: these are estimates.
```

**Fix:**

```text
## Large environment: 6,400 managed names

Indicative tiers for the environment below. Caveat: these are estimates.
```

Three changes: estate to environment, bands to tiers, the timeline
sentence deleted, and the honesty word removed while the estimate label
stays.

## Required agent behavior

1. Before finishing any authored file, search it for band, estate,
   honest, honesty, and duration phrases such as "months to", "weeks to",
   "by Q". Fix every hit.
2. Apply the same check to commit bodies and chat replies.
3. Mirror this file to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` in the same change set
   ([`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)). The workspace
   `.cursor` directory is not a git root.

